WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.960
Today, we're going to conclude the second module of this class about robot perception.

00:12.960 --> 00:17.720
And in particular, we're going to focus on the fourth step of the image processing pipeline.

00:17.720 --> 00:25.440
That is the step of extracting information that can be used for either say localization,

00:25.440 --> 00:31.760
as we will see next week, or for a higher level scene understanding in order to inform

00:31.760 --> 00:32.760
decision making.

00:32.760 --> 00:37.400
Is an object a human or an animal and so on and so forth.

00:37.400 --> 00:42.800
So the goal today is to learn how to extract information from sensor measurements, in particular

00:42.800 --> 00:44.480
from cameras.

00:44.480 --> 00:51.240
And the main reference for this lecture is chapter four from the book from Siguar Urbancash

00:51.240 --> 00:52.240
at Scaramuzza.

00:52.240 --> 01:03.480
So as I said, the goal is to extract information from one or more sensors in order to generate

01:03.480 --> 01:09.960
a higher level percept that can be used for localization or for decision making.

01:09.960 --> 01:18.280
Now, it's important to realize that autonomous robots do not always need to have a very good

01:18.280 --> 01:21.360
understanding of the world around them.

01:21.360 --> 01:27.080
And actually, there is a trade-off between how much understanding you want to have and

01:27.080 --> 01:30.480
the speed and agility of the robots.

01:30.480 --> 01:35.240
If you have a robot that is flying at very high speeds, maybe everything that you need

01:35.240 --> 01:38.160
is just to have a geometric understanding of the world.

01:38.160 --> 01:44.320
But you really don't need to reason about the fine details of what you have around the

01:44.320 --> 01:45.320
robots.

01:45.320 --> 01:48.840
On the other hand, if you want to manipulate the world, for example, if you want to pour

01:48.840 --> 01:54.800
water into glass and sun, then you need to have a much more advanced understanding of

01:54.800 --> 01:55.800
the world around you.

01:55.800 --> 02:00.120
You need to know what is the relation of an object with respect to another one in order

02:00.120 --> 02:04.840
to understand the sequence of tasks that are needed in order to accomplish your task.

02:04.840 --> 02:13.800
Having said that, on the topic of information extraction, we distinguish between low level

02:13.800 --> 02:21.480
features, basically, geometric features, such as lines or circles and sun, that can

02:21.480 --> 02:30.240
be used to form localization, the most basic capability for a robot in order to move around.

02:30.240 --> 02:38.120
And then we consider higher level features, such as recognizing a table or recognizing

02:38.120 --> 02:43.060
an object, which are useful for higher level understanding, which are useful for task planning.

02:43.060 --> 02:46.940
If the robot has to do something that is more advanced than just moving around.

02:46.940 --> 02:56.220
For example, accomplish complex tasks, such as prepare a dish for you or even drive around

02:56.220 --> 02:58.900
a city and so on.

02:58.900 --> 03:07.340
So today we're going to look at both topics, so how to detect geometric primitives in the

03:07.340 --> 03:15.700
environment, and also how to detect objects and hence have a more detailed understanding

03:15.700 --> 03:17.660
of a scene.

03:17.660 --> 03:20.660
Let's start with the geometric primitives.

03:20.660 --> 03:27.900
Now, the typical geometric primitives that one may want to extract, as I said before,

03:27.900 --> 03:36.200
are typically lines or circles, or in 3D, for example, walls, ceilings, and so on.

03:36.200 --> 03:43.080
The two key problems in order to extract geometric primitives are, for example, in

03:43.080 --> 03:49.080
the case of line segmentation, in the case of line extraction, which points belong to

03:49.080 --> 03:50.080
which line?

03:50.080 --> 03:51.760
That's a problem of segmentation.

03:51.760 --> 03:57.040
And given associations of points to a line, how do we estimate the parameters of the line

03:57.040 --> 03:59.400
that best fits those points?

03:59.400 --> 04:05.840
Now, in this class, we are going to focus specifically on line extraction, particularly

04:05.840 --> 04:10.080
because this is one of the most basic geometric primitives that you want to extract.

04:10.080 --> 04:14.240
And indeed, this is a geometric primitive that you will be extracting for your final

04:14.240 --> 04:17.480
project in order to achieve localization.

04:17.480 --> 04:23.000
Accordingly, problem two in your pset focuses on line extraction.

04:23.000 --> 04:28.320
As I said, this is what you'll be using for your final project.

04:28.320 --> 04:32.200
As I said, and this is true basically for all geometric primitives, there is a problem

04:32.200 --> 04:36.900
of segmentation, which points belong to a given geometric primitive, like a line.

04:36.900 --> 04:42.360
And two, once you have found that association, how you find that the geometric primitive

04:42.360 --> 04:47.260
that best explains the points that have been associated to that primitive.

04:47.260 --> 04:50.000
So we're going to look at these two steps in turn.

04:50.000 --> 04:55.560
And in particular, we're going to look at the second step first, which is easier.

04:55.560 --> 04:59.800
And then we'll look later on at the first step.

04:59.800 --> 05:01.800
So let's start with the second step.

05:01.800 --> 05:06.680
The problem of fitting, in particular in the case of a line in 2D.

05:06.680 --> 05:12.200
Similar arguments generalize to 3D, for example for planes, but just for simplicity for this

05:12.200 --> 05:15.120
class we focus on lines.

05:15.120 --> 05:20.880
Now everybody in view knows what an equation of a line is.

05:20.880 --> 05:28.240
So if you are in 2D, this is x, this is y, and you have a line, the equation of a line

05:28.240 --> 05:32.040
in general is y equals mx plus b.

05:32.040 --> 05:35.040
So that's a usual equation of a line.

05:35.040 --> 05:42.360
Typically for image processing, we use a different parametrization of a line, and in particular

05:42.360 --> 05:45.480
the parametrization of a line with respect to polar coordinates.

05:45.480 --> 05:49.240
This is something that may not be familiar with.

05:49.240 --> 05:55.040
So let's explain what a line in the polar coordinates look like.

05:55.040 --> 06:00.960
So you already know about polar coordinates, we saw them earlier in this class.

06:00.960 --> 06:04.160
Just to make sure that everyone is on the same page.

06:04.160 --> 06:08.520
So let's assume that you have a point in the Euclidean plane.

06:08.520 --> 06:15.240
The coordinates you are used to are the x and y coordinates.

06:15.240 --> 06:22.960
But a different way to pinpoint what that point is on the plane is in terms of the distance

06:22.960 --> 06:42.960
of that point from the origin, which we denote as rho, and the angle theta that the segment

06:42.960 --> 06:48.240
joining the point to the origin forms with the x-axis.

06:48.240 --> 06:52.760
If I tell you rho and if I tell you theta, you know exactly what the point is.

06:52.760 --> 07:02.840
Which means that rho and theta are valid coordinates in order to describe each point in the plane.

07:02.840 --> 07:13.800
So then the question is, we know how to describe a line in Cartesian coordinates.

07:13.800 --> 07:18.280
How do we describe a line with respect to the coordinates rho, theta?

07:19.120 --> 07:29.040
Assuming that we have a line whose points are all identified by polar coordinates rho, theta,

07:29.040 --> 07:33.760
what are the parameters that identify this line?

07:33.760 --> 07:46.120
In order to do so, let's consider the plot that we also have over there.

07:46.120 --> 07:56.280
So, we're again given the plane, then we're given a line.

07:56.280 --> 08:05.040
So let's call it B0 and alpha.

08:05.040 --> 08:18.280
So, as you can see, a line is uniquely identified by the distance between the origin and the point

08:18.280 --> 08:24.680
that the segment orthogonal to the line from the origin intersects the line,

08:24.680 --> 08:26.680
and the angle of that segment.

08:26.680 --> 08:29.480
Let's call the length of the segment r.

08:29.480 --> 08:35.400
So here the angle is 90 degrees.

08:35.400 --> 08:41.400
So if I tell you the length of the segment, again this is the segment from the origin to the line

08:41.400 --> 08:46.320
that makes an angle equal to 90 degrees with the line.

08:46.320 --> 08:53.680
If I tell you this distance and if I tell you this angle, I uniquely identify this line.

08:53.680 --> 09:02.600
Now then the question is, what is the relation between all the points on this line

09:02.600 --> 09:07.120
with these two parameters rho, sorry, r and alpha?

09:07.120 --> 09:10.880
That will give us the equation of a line in polar coordinates.

09:10.880 --> 09:19.960
In particular, let's take a general point along the line with the coordinates rho and theta.

09:19.960 --> 09:23.480
What's the relation between rho and theta and r and alpha?

09:23.480 --> 09:28.400
This is a similar equation that we will have for what's the relation between the arbitrary point x, y

09:28.400 --> 09:33.400
on the line and the parameters of the line m and b.

09:33.400 --> 09:38.120
We already know that this relation is y equal mx plus b.

09:38.120 --> 09:44.800
We want to find a similar relation between rho, theta and r and alpha.

09:44.800 --> 09:49.520
Does it make sense?

09:49.560 --> 09:58.800
This length is rho and this angle is theta, all of this.

09:58.800 --> 10:06.960
This angle, the smaller one, this is the theta minus alpha.

10:06.960 --> 10:16.360
By using trigonometry, rho times cosine of this angle is equal to r.

10:16.360 --> 10:25.400
So rho cosine of theta minus alpha is equal to r.

10:25.400 --> 10:34.520
And this is valid for every point on the line described with respect to polar coordinates.

10:34.520 --> 10:44.600
So we can then define a line with parameters r and alpha as the locus of points such that

10:44.640 --> 10:49.200
those points, when described in polar coordinates, satisfy this equation.

10:49.200 --> 10:53.400
Similar to what you would do in the standard Cartesian coordinates,

10:53.400 --> 11:01.160
a line is the locus of points such that those points described in Cartesian coordinates satisfy this equation.

11:01.160 --> 11:05.680
Does this make sense?

11:05.680 --> 11:07.360
You don't seem convinced.

11:07.360 --> 11:08.880
You are convinced.

11:08.880 --> 11:10.760
So-so.

11:10.760 --> 11:12.960
So-so or yes?

11:13.000 --> 11:14.120
OK, cool.

11:14.120 --> 11:16.640
All right.

11:16.640 --> 11:33.240
So again, view this as the polar coordinate representation of y equal mx plus v.

11:33.240 --> 11:41.360
Equivalently, by using the, and this will become useful later on,

11:41.360 --> 11:51.000
by using the fact that cosine of alpha minus beta is equal to cosine of alpha cosine of beta

11:51.000 --> 11:56.480
plus sine of alpha sine of beta.

11:56.480 --> 12:05.360
This equation can also be written as rho cosine of theta cosine of alpha

12:05.400 --> 12:13.040
plus rho sine of theta sine of alpha.

12:13.040 --> 12:15.040
So this is what?

12:15.040 --> 12:18.600
Rho cosine of theta?

12:18.600 --> 12:19.840
Yep.

12:19.840 --> 12:28.520
So it's x cosine of alpha, and this is y sine of alpha equal r.

12:28.520 --> 12:33.240
So basically, this is how we go from the equation on the line in polar coordinates

12:33.240 --> 12:38.720
to the equation on the line in xy coordinates.

12:38.720 --> 12:39.600
Cool, all right.

12:39.600 --> 12:45.440
So all this to say that in polar coordinates,

12:45.440 --> 12:51.240
a line is described in terms of the distance between the point p0, which

12:51.240 --> 12:58.600
is the intersection between the line from the origin to the line orthogonal,

12:58.600 --> 13:02.600
to our line orthogonal to that line, and the angle that that segment forms.

13:06.080 --> 13:12.040
OK, so we know we have now endpoints.

13:12.040 --> 13:12.960
Yes?

13:12.960 --> 13:19.400
How do we deal with lines that go through the origin point in this construct?

13:19.400 --> 13:20.840
You mean here?

13:20.840 --> 13:27.320
This will be done with r equal to, yeah, this will be not well-defined,

13:27.320 --> 13:28.920
because we will not have alpha.

13:28.920 --> 13:36.400
But we have that singularity also with the case of,

13:36.400 --> 13:41.840
because for example here, m could go all the way to infinity.

13:41.840 --> 13:45.800
Yeah, so it's, but as we will see later,

13:45.800 --> 13:50.840
this representation has an advantage with respect to the Cartesian advantage.

13:50.880 --> 13:57.880
In particular, m is a number that can go from 0 to potentially infinity.

13:57.880 --> 14:01.280
So when we try to estimate from data this m,

14:01.280 --> 14:04.360
we have to account for an infinite interval from the slope.

14:04.360 --> 14:08.200
Instead, in the case of the polar coordinate representation,

14:08.200 --> 14:11.480
alpha is obviously between 0 and 2 pi.

14:11.480 --> 14:17.680
And r is basically the distance of the line from the origin.

14:17.680 --> 14:21.440
But since you have a bound in terms of what the environment is,

14:21.440 --> 14:24.560
r is always a well-defined number.

14:24.560 --> 14:26.960
And this is something that we'll see later on.

14:26.960 --> 14:29.520
But that's a good point.

14:29.520 --> 14:30.400
Cool, all right.

14:30.400 --> 14:33.880
So now, assume that, yeah, here.

14:33.880 --> 14:39.720
Assume that now we have endpoints, rho i, theta i.

14:48.320 --> 14:52.320
So we have n of those points.

14:52.320 --> 14:55.800
And we know that they all belong to the same line.

14:55.800 --> 15:00.960
So there exists an r and alpha such that

15:00.960 --> 15:07.600
rho i cosine of theta i minus alpha is equal to r.

15:07.600 --> 15:10.200
So I give you these endpoints.

15:10.200 --> 15:12.160
You know that they are on the same line.

15:12.160 --> 15:14.880
We want to find r and alpha.

15:14.880 --> 15:17.200
So this will be a system of equations.

15:17.200 --> 15:20.320
Nonlinear, but this is a system of equations that you can solve for.

15:20.320 --> 15:26.160
Unfortunately, due to noise, which could be due to measurement noise,

15:26.160 --> 15:32.680
outliers, and so on, even if these points in reality belong to the same line,

15:32.680 --> 15:39.320
what we will measure is something that has some residual, some error.

15:39.320 --> 15:42.640
So the best we can do in order to estimate the line,

15:42.640 --> 15:45.560
meaning its coefficients r and alpha,

15:45.600 --> 15:53.080
is to find the coefficients r and alpha that best describe

15:53.080 --> 16:01.080
how the points will fit at that line in terms of minimizing the residual error.

16:01.080 --> 16:08.480
Specifically, what we do is to form a cost function.

16:15.760 --> 16:18.240
Which is a function of the parameters of the line.

16:18.240 --> 16:20.520
These are the two parameters we want to find,

16:20.520 --> 16:23.880
which is equal to the summation from i equal to one to n,

16:23.880 --> 16:27.160
the total number of points, over d i square.

16:27.160 --> 16:37.640
The discrepancy between the equation of the line and what we actually have.

16:37.640 --> 16:46.840
This is just the discrepancy in r and not the discrepancy in alpha.

16:46.840 --> 16:57.600
Yeah, well, this d i basically accounts for the distance of the point

16:57.600 --> 16:59.360
with respect to the desired line.

16:59.360 --> 17:01.640
That's the meaning of...

17:01.640 --> 17:06.280
So basically, we're trying to find the line that best explains the point

17:06.280 --> 17:11.080
by minimizing the orthogonal distance between that line

17:11.080 --> 17:15.000
and each one of those points, summing all those errors.

17:15.000 --> 17:17.080
This is a model, obviously.

17:17.080 --> 17:22.120
But usually, the most common model for line fitting.

17:22.120 --> 17:35.000
So then the problem is to minimize over r and alpha this cost function.

17:35.000 --> 17:42.880
Which you can see as a least squares problem without constraints.

17:42.880 --> 17:48.840
So here, we are assuming that we equally weight all the measurements.

17:48.840 --> 17:55.480
Remember that the measurements are the values rho i's,

17:55.480 --> 17:59.560
referred to as the range or the distance to each point,

17:59.560 --> 18:02.200
something that a Lidar sensor would give you.

18:02.200 --> 18:07.920
And the theta i, which is the bearing with respect to that point.

18:07.920 --> 18:08.840
This is what you measure.

18:08.840 --> 18:14.200
You want to find the parameters r and alpha that best explain

18:14.200 --> 18:16.560
those measurements in terms of belonging to a line.

18:16.560 --> 18:18.040
Yeah.

18:18.040 --> 18:22.800
You're saying that there's no noise on alpha in this model?

18:22.800 --> 18:27.440
Well, implicitly, you also can't afford noise in...

18:27.440 --> 18:31.440
So the noise is really...

18:31.440 --> 18:34.800
We assume that we have noise in both...

18:34.800 --> 18:41.280
So first of all, the noise is neither in alpha nor in r.

18:41.280 --> 18:44.400
So these are just the parameter of the underlying line.

18:44.400 --> 18:49.040
The noise is in rho and theta, which are the quantities that we are measuring.

18:49.040 --> 18:53.400
So we assume that because of the noise in rho and theta,

18:53.400 --> 19:00.200
the end result will be a point that is offset with respect to the line.

19:00.200 --> 19:04.200
And then, in order to find the line that best fits those points,

19:04.200 --> 19:11.160
we try to minimize the error in terms of the distance of each point from that line.

19:11.160 --> 19:14.920
So in a standard Cartesian measurement system,

19:14.920 --> 19:19.000
you kind of assume there's a Gaussian error about x and a Gaussian error around y.

19:19.000 --> 19:23.400
And then you do the fitting of a line with these squares,

19:23.400 --> 19:26.840
and it minimizes those errors in some good way.

19:26.840 --> 19:31.160
And here, we're working with the...

19:31.160 --> 19:33.480
We're not only measuring things using a LiDAR sensor, right?

19:33.480 --> 19:35.240
Which has error in alpha and...

19:35.240 --> 19:39.560
Yeah, typically with the LiDAR sensor, so you measure distance.

19:39.560 --> 19:41.400
Does that still get you...

19:41.400 --> 19:46.920
Do errors in alpha and distance still translate into Gaussian errors in x and y?

19:46.920 --> 19:48.200
Yeah.

19:48.920 --> 19:50.360
Yeah, that's a good point.

19:50.360 --> 19:51.320
The answer is yes.

19:51.320 --> 19:55.000
And we could actually do an error propagation analysis

19:55.000 --> 20:00.600
to see how those errors propagate into the final estimates that you compute.

20:00.600 --> 20:02.520
But we don't do it.

20:02.520 --> 20:03.800
Because the equation on the next slide...

20:05.800 --> 20:09.400
That minimization equation is minimizing for the Gaussian in xy, right?

20:11.960 --> 20:13.880
There's no real notion of xy.

20:13.880 --> 20:20.680
Here, we are really minimizing the distance between a point and the line.

20:23.480 --> 20:26.920
So the question is how the Gaussians with respect to rho and theta

20:26.920 --> 20:30.120
transform into a Gaussian for that distance.

20:30.120 --> 20:31.800
That's what we are minimizing.

20:31.800 --> 20:33.000
Which basically leads...

20:33.000 --> 20:37.800
So we could actually do a more sophisticated stochastic analysis

20:37.800 --> 20:40.520
of the foundation of what we're doing here.

20:41.160 --> 20:43.160
But we don't do it for simplicity.

20:43.160 --> 20:45.000
Also, it's not that important.

20:45.000 --> 20:47.480
But what is important to acknowledge, as you were mentioning,

20:48.120 --> 20:53.400
is that there is an underlying notion of uncertainty behind each measurement.

20:53.400 --> 20:58.520
And in particular, we know, for example, for stereo ranging,

20:59.160 --> 21:04.920
that measurements of points that are farther away have more uncertainty.

21:04.920 --> 21:08.520
In particular, the variance increases with the square of the distance.

21:08.520 --> 21:16.040
Which prompts us to actually weight the different measurements.

21:16.040 --> 21:20.040
Basically give more weight to those measurements that are more certain.

21:20.600 --> 21:23.080
So points that are closer to us,

21:23.080 --> 21:25.720
and less weight to those measurements that are farther away.

21:26.360 --> 21:32.680
Practically, what we do is to set up weights wi equal to one over sigma i square.

21:32.680 --> 21:34.920
That is exactly what you're referring to.

21:34.920 --> 21:41.640
The uncertainty in the measurement that we have for a point.

21:42.920 --> 21:47.640
And consider then the weighted cost function.

21:48.280 --> 21:52.600
Whereby, if you see, if we are highly uncertain about a measurement,

21:52.600 --> 21:54.360
meaning that sigma is high,

21:54.360 --> 21:57.720
then we give less weight for that measurement

21:57.720 --> 22:00.520
when we do the least square fitting procedure.

22:00.520 --> 22:05.720
This is still a least square problem,

22:05.720 --> 22:07.880
it's a weighted least square problem.

