25:02.200 --> 25:03.080
You get rho and theta.

25:03.080 --> 25:05.880
So you could do the transformation, obviously.

25:05.880 --> 25:10.040
But it's better to just go straight from the measurement to the line fitting.

25:10.600 --> 25:14.600
Three, I would use the polar representation of a line

25:15.720 --> 25:19.880
in order to also solve the segmentation problem by using Q-transform,

25:19.880 --> 25:22.120
which we're going to discuss in a few slides.

25:22.120 --> 25:27.800
So just for ease of explanation, I prefer to do everything in polar coordinates.

25:28.520 --> 25:31.240
But yes, in principle, you could do it in xy as well.

25:34.280 --> 25:35.640
Does that address your question?

25:35.640 --> 25:36.280
Yeah.

25:36.280 --> 25:39.320
So it's a mixture of pedagogical reason,

25:40.520 --> 25:43.000
the type of measurements that you actually have,

25:43.000 --> 25:45.960
and the fact that this is a little bit more robust way of doing it.

25:45.960 --> 25:48.680
All right.

25:48.680 --> 25:51.160
Let's go to step number one, light segmentation.

25:51.160 --> 25:53.880
So far, we have assumed that we have a bunch of points.

25:53.880 --> 25:56.120
We know that they all belong to a line.

25:56.120 --> 25:58.520
And then we want to find the coefficients of that line.

25:58.520 --> 26:01.720
We use polar coordinates for a number of reasons that I just explained.

26:01.720 --> 26:03.720
Of course, if you're really against polar coordinates,

26:03.720 --> 26:05.320
then you can use Cartesian coordinates.

26:06.920 --> 26:10.760
The first problem is, OK, but who tells you whether

26:10.760 --> 26:14.040
a bunch of points belong to a given line?

26:14.040 --> 26:20.120
What you get from lighters are a bunch of rho i's, theta i's,

26:20.120 --> 26:21.000
which is very large.

26:22.120 --> 26:24.120
Which point belongs to which line?

26:25.560 --> 26:26.520
And that's tricky.

26:26.520 --> 26:29.000
And that's very important because, of course, for localization,

26:29.000 --> 26:33.000
you want to understand whether that wall is the same as that wall

26:33.000 --> 26:36.520
or there are two different walls with an angle between them.

26:36.520 --> 26:40.840
So we're going to look at the three main methods for line segmentation.

26:41.640 --> 26:47.880
They are relatively simple, but still require a little bit of explanation.

26:48.440 --> 26:51.960
So the first one, which is really really simple,

26:51.960 --> 26:54.280
is referred to as a split and merge.

26:55.560 --> 26:58.520
The deal of split and merge is best illustrated by,

27:01.400 --> 27:06.360
through my skillful PowerPoint drawing skills.

27:07.400 --> 27:09.960
So let's assume that we have a bunch of points.

27:09.960 --> 27:11.240
Now it's a segmentation problem.

27:11.240 --> 27:13.720
We don't know which point belongs to which line.

27:13.720 --> 27:17.720
As a human, you will say, OK, probably this is a line

27:17.720 --> 27:19.000
and that this is a line.

27:19.000 --> 27:21.000
This is what we want to do with the algorithm.

27:21.000 --> 27:23.000
All right, so we had this bunch of points.

27:23.000 --> 27:27.800
So the first thing that we do is just to fit a line

27:27.800 --> 27:31.080
through all of these points by using the same algorithm

27:31.080 --> 27:32.840
that I explained before.

27:32.840 --> 27:36.600
So we assume, OK, maybe all of them belong to the single line.

27:36.600 --> 27:37.480
And that's what we do.

27:37.480 --> 27:39.640
We fit this line.

27:40.760 --> 27:49.800
Then we look at the point that has a maximum distance from the line.

27:49.800 --> 27:55.160
In this case, this will be the probably, probably this one.

27:55.160 --> 27:58.120
This will be the point that has a maximum distance to the line.

27:59.800 --> 28:05.640
So then we say, OK, if this maximum distance is above a design threshold,

28:06.360 --> 28:10.200
say one centimeter or one millimeter, that's a threshold that you decide.

28:11.080 --> 28:13.640
If this distance is above the threshold, then we say, OK,

28:13.640 --> 28:17.000
this point cannot really belong to that line,

28:17.000 --> 28:22.200
which means that I have to split this line into two lines.

28:23.080 --> 28:26.680
So one that will go through this set of points

28:26.680 --> 28:28.680
and the other one that goes through that set of points.

28:31.480 --> 28:32.520
So that's what we do.

28:32.520 --> 28:33.400
And then we iterate.

28:33.400 --> 28:34.840
So this is an iterative algorithm.

28:34.840 --> 28:36.920
Now I have this subset of points.

28:36.920 --> 28:38.760
I redraw a line.

28:38.760 --> 28:39.560
I compute.

28:40.200 --> 28:44.120
I find the point that has maximum distance with respect to the line.

28:44.120 --> 28:45.000
In this case, this one.

28:45.720 --> 28:49.480
And I say, is this distance less than the acceptable threshold?

28:49.480 --> 28:50.680
In this case, it is.

28:50.680 --> 28:53.880
So then I freeze this set of points as belonging to the same line.

28:54.440 --> 28:55.720
I go to this other one.

28:55.720 --> 28:59.080
And again, here are all the points past the threshold test.

28:59.080 --> 29:00.040
And then I'm done.

29:00.040 --> 29:00.540
Yeah?

29:00.540 --> 29:03.240
So you identified the one that was furthest away from the first line.

29:03.240 --> 29:03.740
Yeah.

29:04.060 --> 29:08.700
But then how did you split the points into the group of three and the group of four?

29:08.700 --> 29:09.200
Yeah.

29:09.200 --> 29:16.380
So basically, I have an order list of the points that I define as, where is it?

29:16.380 --> 29:16.880
L?

29:17.820 --> 29:18.780
And then they're indexed.

29:19.820 --> 29:22.300
So I use that indexing in order to split them.

29:22.300 --> 29:24.620
So there are the indexing along the line?

29:24.620 --> 29:25.120
Yeah.

29:25.120 --> 29:26.060
Not along the line.

29:26.060 --> 29:30.300
So basically, at the beginning, I just have a bunch of points.

29:30.300 --> 29:31.980
And I just put them in an array.

29:32.700 --> 29:33.200
OK.

29:33.200 --> 29:35.980
So just hide them with this, because, OK.

29:35.980 --> 29:36.480
Oh, yeah.

29:36.480 --> 29:42.140
See, this is a very carefully constructed case, so that the iteration stops, right?

29:42.140 --> 29:42.640
No, no.

29:42.640 --> 29:43.180
You're right.

29:43.180 --> 29:47.420
In general, I don't have this very nice sequencing and this very nice pattern.

29:47.420 --> 29:52.620
Is there such a way that you have to do and split the points in the not correct way?

29:53.180 --> 29:58.460
Well, but then you will keep splitting until you don't have these weird things.

29:58.460 --> 30:00.300
There is another step.

30:00.780 --> 30:02.300
That's the bridge rule.

30:02.300 --> 30:02.800
Exactly.

30:03.740 --> 30:08.540
So I have another example that maybe addresses that a little bit better.

30:08.540 --> 30:10.700
So this is a refinement of split and merge

30:10.700 --> 30:13.740
that is referred to as an iterative endpoint fit variant.

30:14.300 --> 30:21.500
So what you do is basically a simplification of the previous algorithm,

30:21.500 --> 30:26.860
whereby instead of doing least squares in order to compute the parameters of the line,

30:26.860 --> 30:27.900
you just merge.

30:27.900 --> 30:32.220
So you just have a bunch of points that you have ordered somehow.

30:33.340 --> 30:39.500
And then you connect the first point according to your indexing to the last point.

30:39.500 --> 30:40.700
In this case, it will be these two.

30:42.140 --> 30:43.420
Same deal as before.

30:43.420 --> 30:48.140
We compute the point that has the farthest distance, and then we split.

30:52.140 --> 30:52.940
And then, oops.

30:53.500 --> 30:57.900
And now for this subset of points, we compute another line

30:59.100 --> 31:03.500
by connecting the first and the last element of that set.

31:03.500 --> 31:05.500
We compute a point that is the farthest away.

31:07.180 --> 31:08.940
And this is, again, about the threshold.

31:08.940 --> 31:09.580
So we split.

31:11.260 --> 31:11.900
I get here.

31:12.540 --> 31:15.100
So here, no more splits are possible.

31:15.660 --> 31:20.060
And then there is a final post-processing step whereby we just merge,

31:20.060 --> 31:21.100
and we have a straight line.

31:23.020 --> 31:26.780
This is exactly the case that because of the splitting procedure,

31:26.780 --> 31:30.300
we introduce two lines that were very similar with each other.

31:30.300 --> 31:34.780
So we connected that a posteriori with the merge procedure.

31:35.900 --> 31:38.220
So of course, there are a lot of moving parameters in these things.

31:38.220 --> 31:40.300
So getting it to work correctly.

31:41.100 --> 31:42.780
So the concept is fairly easy.

31:43.420 --> 31:46.380
But getting it to work correctly is a bit hairy.

31:46.380 --> 31:49.100
And you have a problem in your pset to do exactly that.

31:49.100 --> 31:56.780
Does the convergence happen based on the amount of threshold you set?

31:56.780 --> 31:57.580
Oh, yeah, of course.

31:58.700 --> 31:59.980
That's what I'm saying is a bit hairy.

31:59.980 --> 32:05.020
Because depending on the threshold, you might get results that are really awkward.

32:05.660 --> 32:08.540
And as you will see in the second problem, the pset.

32:08.540 --> 32:10.940
So yeah, that requires a little bit of experience.

32:10.940 --> 32:14.620
And the iteration of the threshold remains the same?

32:15.260 --> 32:15.980
Typically, yes.

32:19.340 --> 32:22.700
This felt like it was really dependent on the indexing.

32:25.820 --> 32:30.220
So how do you go about choosing the right index?

32:30.220 --> 32:34.700
Or how do you go about constructing that initial array?

32:34.700 --> 32:36.220
Yeah, there are a lot of moving.

32:36.220 --> 32:38.620
There are a lot of algorithm decisions.

32:38.620 --> 32:44.460
So one, for example, would be to index them in terms of their range distance.

32:45.340 --> 32:49.820
Or you just put it back in terms of where they are in the image.

32:49.820 --> 32:52.940
Basically, there are countless variations of how you can do it.

32:52.940 --> 32:55.180
We provide you with an example in the pset2.

32:55.820 --> 32:56.860
But you're right.

32:56.860 --> 32:59.180
So as I said, the idea is simple.

32:59.180 --> 33:01.420
But making it work correctly is not so simple.

33:01.980 --> 33:07.420
Which actually give rise to the second algorithm that I want to mention, RANSAC.

33:07.420 --> 33:11.340
This is really super simple to implement and super robust.

33:11.420 --> 33:13.260
And actually, it's one of my favorite algorithms.

33:13.980 --> 33:16.860
It's simple, as I said, very robust.

33:17.500 --> 33:19.340
RANSAC, indeed, is an idea.

33:19.340 --> 33:23.420
So RANSAC, first of all, stands for random sample consensus.

33:24.220 --> 33:29.580
And it's a very general algorithm that allows you to estimate

33:29.580 --> 33:33.900
in a robust way parameters of a model, even in the presence of outliers.

33:33.900 --> 33:40.780
So I'm using RANSAC for the specific purpose of segmenting points on the line.

33:41.740 --> 33:44.940
But RANSAC can be used much more generally

33:44.940 --> 33:49.500
in order to find the correspondences between points across different images,

33:49.500 --> 33:53.020
finding planes in the 3D images, and so on.

33:53.020 --> 33:55.580
So the RANSAC concept, per se, is very, very general.

33:57.580 --> 34:01.660
So for example, when you want to do stereo matching,

34:01.660 --> 34:07.020
you have to find the correspondences between two images.

34:07.660 --> 34:13.260
Then most of the image processing pipeline out there

34:13.260 --> 34:15.740
use RANSAC in order to reject outliers.

34:15.740 --> 34:18.060
And we'll see in a second how that works.

34:18.860 --> 34:25.020
But anyway, the idea of RANSAC is to use an iterative randomized method

34:25.020 --> 34:27.900
in order to estimate parameters by rejecting outliers.

34:28.540 --> 34:32.780
Let's look at how it works for the case of line segmentation.

34:33.580 --> 34:35.260
So we have a bunch of points here.

34:35.820 --> 34:40.220
As a human, you might argue that this probably corresponds to a line.

34:40.780 --> 34:41.980
This maybe is also a line.

34:44.060 --> 34:47.020
So the way that the RANSAC algorithm works is extremely simple.

34:47.660 --> 34:51.660
You take two points, and then you compute the line

34:51.660 --> 34:53.180
that goes through those two points.

34:53.180 --> 34:56.380
Of course, there is only one line that goes through those two points.

34:56.940 --> 34:59.500
And then you compute the number of outliers,

34:59.500 --> 35:03.340
meaning the numbers of a point that are within a distance,

35:03.340 --> 35:05.980
how do we call it here, gamma maybe?

35:06.940 --> 35:10.780
Number that go, yeah, that are within a distance gamma from that line.

35:12.620 --> 35:14.380
And then I select the other two points,

35:14.380 --> 35:16.300
and then I compute the number of outliers.

35:16.300 --> 35:19.020
And I do that a certain number of times.

35:19.020 --> 35:22.860
And at the end, I choose the line with the maximum number of outliers.

35:23.580 --> 35:26.620
So the way it works, as I said, you take two points,

35:26.620 --> 35:29.100
like for example, this red point here and this red point here.

35:30.380 --> 35:31.900
You compute the line that goes through them.

35:32.780 --> 35:34.380
You compute the outliers,

35:36.140 --> 35:39.900
meaning those points whose distance is below a given threshold.

35:39.900 --> 35:41.500
So here I'm computing all the distances.

35:42.140 --> 35:45.900
I only keep the points whose distance is below a given threshold.

35:45.900 --> 35:47.260
This is a set of outliers.

35:47.260 --> 35:51.020
So for example, here I have one, two, three, four, six, seven, eight, nine.

35:51.660 --> 35:53.420
So this line has nine outliers.

35:55.420 --> 35:57.900
I do it for these other two points.

35:57.900 --> 36:00.780
And here I have three, seven, outliers.

36:01.500 --> 36:02.300
Keep doing that.

36:02.300 --> 36:03.180
Keep doing that.

36:03.180 --> 36:06.060
Until I'm lucky, I pick these two points,

36:06.060 --> 36:07.900
and I get a big number of outliers.

36:10.060 --> 36:14.860
At the end, I choose the line that has the largest number of outliers.

36:15.580 --> 36:19.180
In principle, in order to be exhaustive,

36:19.180 --> 36:21.580
you should consider all possible pairs.

36:22.940 --> 36:26.940
So you have, assume that you have in total n points.

36:26.940 --> 36:29.420
So how many pairs you will need to consider in total?

36:31.020 --> 36:32.060
The answer is already there.

36:32.780 --> 36:35.820
Is n times n minus 1 over 2,

36:35.820 --> 36:39.820
because of course, the order with which you pick the first point doesn't matter.

36:42.460 --> 36:44.700
Which in general is very large.

36:45.900 --> 36:49.180
So the idea of RANSAC is that you,

36:49.180 --> 36:52.460
and again, this is a very general idea that goes beyond the line fitting.

36:52.460 --> 36:56.460
It's not that you have to select all possible combinations,

36:57.020 --> 37:00.540
but if you have a little bit of a prior information

37:00.540 --> 37:06.300
in terms of how many outliers you should expect for a given geometric primitive,

37:06.300 --> 37:09.420
you can dramatically decrease the number of points that you need to consider.

37:10.220 --> 37:11.020
How?

37:11.020 --> 37:16.060
Let's assume that we have at least a rough estimate

37:17.180 --> 37:26.140
of the percentage number of outliers that a geometric primitive will have.

37:26.780 --> 37:30.620
For example, if you look at a line that is one meter long,

37:30.620 --> 37:33.740
and you have a given resolution for your slider,

37:33.740 --> 37:37.820
you maybe have a good sense that maybe the number of outliers should be maybe 100.

37:38.380 --> 37:39.980
So 100 total outliers,

37:39.980 --> 37:41.580
if you have one million points,

37:41.580 --> 37:43.660
that gives you the fraction of a layer that you should expect.

37:44.860 --> 37:47.980
So this is something that oftentimes you have.

37:47.980 --> 37:51.020
Even if you don't have, there are more advanced versions of RANSAC

37:51.020 --> 37:54.060
that are capable of identifying w,

37:54.060 --> 37:57.580
so the percentage of outliers in an adaptive way.

37:57.580 --> 38:02.060
So basically you will learn it as you proceed with RANSAC.

38:02.780 --> 38:06.060
So in particular, let's assume that w, the fraction of outliers,

38:06.060 --> 38:10.780
is something that we either know or we have a good estimate of it.

38:11.980 --> 38:14.060
So w is a fraction of outliers.

38:15.580 --> 38:18.140
And then let's assume that, so this is a randomized algorithm,

38:18.140 --> 38:21.420
let's assume that we have a desired probability

38:22.300 --> 38:26.860
of finding a set of points that is free of outliers.

38:26.860 --> 38:29.580
And that probability p, for example, could be 99%.

38:32.620 --> 38:35.980
So we want to run RANSAC a number of times

38:35.980 --> 38:38.380
such that with probability at least 99%,

38:39.260 --> 38:41.980
I select two points that are outliers.

38:44.860 --> 38:46.460
And this is again a design parameter.

38:46.460 --> 38:49.100
You can set it at 99.9% as you want.

38:49.740 --> 38:54.780
The question is, given the knowledge of the fraction of outliers,

38:55.500 --> 39:02.860
given the desired probability of finding two points that are indeed outliers,

39:03.420 --> 39:07.500
what is the number of times that I need to run RANSAC

39:07.500 --> 39:10.860
in order to find two outliers with probability at least 99%?

39:14.380 --> 39:17.740
The reason why we're doing that is because we want to run RANSAC

39:17.820 --> 39:21.660
a number of times that is much lower than capital N square.

39:23.500 --> 39:28.140
To answer that, we just need to do a little bit of probability.

39:29.020 --> 39:35.580
So the probability of selecting two outliers,

39:35.580 --> 39:37.500
so two points that belong to that line,

39:38.860 --> 39:44.140
assuming that the way we select points is independent,

39:44.140 --> 39:47.260
is W squared.

39:47.260 --> 39:50.140
Both points in LIGR.

39:53.500 --> 39:54.000
Yep?

39:59.420 --> 40:00.780
No, no, we don't know what the line is.

40:01.740 --> 40:03.180
So we know that there is a line.

40:03.180 --> 40:04.460
I mean, we assume that there is a line.

40:05.180 --> 40:08.060
And we assume that there are some outliers along that line.

40:08.060 --> 40:09.500
But we don't know which of them...

40:14.380 --> 40:16.460
Yes, we're trying to find the line.

40:17.180 --> 40:19.660
So we assume that there is a line in the environment,

40:19.660 --> 40:21.980
and with RANSAC we're trying to find it.

40:23.260 --> 40:26.700
It might be the case that after we run RANSAC a number of times,

40:26.700 --> 40:29.500
we find a number of outliers that is very small,

40:29.500 --> 40:32.860
which means that basically there is no line in your environment that is fine too.

40:33.820 --> 40:35.420
But the question is, if there is a line,

40:36.380 --> 40:40.540
can we find it after running RANSAC a given number of times

40:40.540 --> 40:41.900
with probability at least 99%?

40:41.900 --> 40:44.780
But of course, if there is no line,

40:44.780 --> 40:46.460
then RANSAC will not find that line.

40:48.380 --> 40:52.220
So the probability that both points are in LIGR is,

40:53.420 --> 40:54.460
when you select them,

40:54.460 --> 40:58.620
the probability that both points are in LIGRs will be W squared.

40:59.740 --> 41:09.660
So the probability that at least one of them is not an inlier,

41:12.460 --> 41:15.580
is equal to 1 minus W squared.

41:16.700 --> 41:20.380
So what is the probability that after k times that you run RANSAC,

41:21.020 --> 41:23.340
you don't pick two points that are in LIGRs?

41:25.180 --> 41:26.380
So the probability...

41:28.460 --> 41:29.500
How do we call it there?

41:29.500 --> 41:30.620
RANSAC never selects...

41:34.060 --> 41:35.740
Okay, RANSAC is unlucky.

41:37.900 --> 41:41.500
Unlucky means never selects two points that are both in LIGRs.

41:41.980 --> 41:46.700
Is 1 minus W squared to the power of k.

41:49.420 --> 41:51.260
Is unlucky after k trials.

41:55.580 --> 41:57.180
1 minus W squared.

41:57.180 --> 41:59.100
The reason why I go through this derivation is that

41:59.100 --> 42:00.620
RANSAC is a very important algorithm.

42:00.620 --> 42:03.740
So besides line fitting, it's used much more broadly.

42:03.740 --> 42:06.780
So I want to make sure that at least you are familiar with the basic concepts.

42:06.780 --> 42:14.540
Yeah, absolutely.

42:14.540 --> 42:17.180
That's why W is a really rough estimate.

42:17.180 --> 42:22.060
It's like a conceptual construct that you use in order to get a sense of

42:22.060 --> 42:24.460
how many iterations you may need.

42:24.460 --> 42:26.620
But yes, you never know W exactly.

42:27.180 --> 42:29.900
You just have some estimate that you use to

42:29.900 --> 42:32.220
guide your selection number of RANSAC iterations.

42:32.220 --> 42:36.540
I know, it seems scary, right?

42:36.540 --> 42:40.300
That the life of a person on an autonomous car depends on this.

42:41.740 --> 42:42.380
But it works.

42:43.580 --> 42:46.940
So that point cloud you showed us, and all the points there is,

42:46.940 --> 42:50.620
you can see as a human that there's a line of points that was denser than the rest of them.

42:50.620 --> 42:54.700
Yes, that means there are more in LIGRs.

42:54.700 --> 42:59.660
Yeah, would it be possible to cut down over points you have to check with RANSAC

42:59.660 --> 43:05.340
by only considering points that are in a certain density threshold, so you don't?

43:06.140 --> 43:06.700
You could.

43:06.700 --> 43:11.100
I mean, you could better inform RANSAC in order to tell RANSAC to focus on some regions.

43:11.740 --> 43:12.620
Certainly true.

43:12.620 --> 43:15.820
I mean, again, RANSAC, you have zillions of variations.

43:15.820 --> 43:17.820
This is really the most basic version of it.

43:18.860 --> 43:20.460
Just based on the version you presented,

43:20.460 --> 43:23.420
won't RANSAC only ever find one line, as you have provided?

43:24.060 --> 43:28.460
Yes, because we just picked the maximum number of layers.

43:28.540 --> 43:31.500
So then what you would do would be to remove all of these,

43:31.500 --> 43:33.660
and then RANSAC again to find that line.

43:33.660 --> 43:34.160
Yeah.

43:35.820 --> 43:39.740
So coming back to the equation of, wow, is this thing works?

43:39.740 --> 43:43.980
