44:06.380 --> 44:08.140
about what there is behind.

44:09.100 --> 44:09.600
Yep.

44:10.620 --> 44:13.260
Anyway, no, but actually this works pretty well in practice.

44:13.260 --> 44:16.460
So even though we make all these approximations, it's a very robust algorithm.

44:16.460 --> 44:23.180
In particular, let's assume that we are confident about the W value.

44:24.140 --> 44:31.500
So the question is how many iterations you need in order to find a couple of outliers,

44:31.500 --> 44:34.140
so a pair of outliers so that actually you have a line.

44:34.140 --> 44:43.260
Well, as k grows larger, this quantity goes monotonically and synthetically to zero,

44:43.260 --> 44:46.140
which means that as k becomes larger and larger,

44:46.140 --> 44:49.100
the probability that you don't get to two points becomes lower and lower.

44:49.820 --> 44:59.740
In particular, you want to find a pair of outliers with a probability of at least 99%, say,

44:59.740 --> 45:05.180
which means that you want the probability of not finding the entire to be less than 1%.

45:06.060 --> 45:17.740
So you run RANSAC until 1 minus p is greater than or equal to 1 minus w square to the power of k.

45:18.460 --> 45:22.060
And you know that there is a k that is large enough such that this is true,

45:22.060 --> 45:25.500
because you know that as k goes to infinity, this goes to zero.

45:30.220 --> 45:35.580
In particular, basically you choose the k such that this is an equality.

45:36.380 --> 45:38.220
It's not really an equality because, of course,

45:42.060 --> 45:46.140
you may not find a k precisely that makes this equal to 1 minus p,

45:46.140 --> 45:48.380
but just for simplicity, let's assume it is an equality.

45:49.260 --> 45:55.580
So you then consider a number of iterations that is equal to log 1 minus p over log 1 minus w

45:55.580 --> 46:01.500
square. Again, this is basically a guideline that you use in order to tune your algorithm.

46:02.140 --> 46:09.420
The important point here is that k bar depends on w but not on n, which means that in general,

46:09.420 --> 46:15.100
with RANSAC, you need a number of iterations that is much lower than the total number of points.

46:15.100 --> 46:19.980
Since with LIDARs you have millions of points, that is a very important concept.

46:20.540 --> 46:26.940
Second of all, there are more advanced versions of RANSAC that actually retune the w in an adaptive

46:26.940 --> 46:37.340
way. All right, any questions here? RANSAC is useful for light fatigue but much more useful

46:37.340 --> 46:41.820
broadly. So if you stick with the field of robot autonomy, it's very likely that you will stumble

46:41.820 --> 46:50.780
upon RANSAC in the future. Third method for segmentation, Q-transform. This is a very cute

46:51.740 --> 46:58.620
idea and also quite widely used. So again, the idea is that we have a bunch of points.

46:58.620 --> 47:03.820
So you see that we have increasingly more refined ways of doing it. Of course, the more refined your

47:03.820 --> 47:09.180
method is, the more computation you need to do. So same problem, we have a bunch of points we want

47:09.180 --> 47:17.580
to find whether there is a line that goes through those points. So the idea of the Q-transform is

47:18.860 --> 47:26.220
simple but very clever. The idea is that, as we discussed before,

47:29.340 --> 47:34.140
a line in Cartesian coordinates is described as y equals mx plus b.

47:35.100 --> 47:44.060
Which means that if I give you a point with coordinates xi and yi,

47:45.740 --> 47:52.620
what is the set of possible lines that can go through that point? It will be this line, this line,

47:52.620 --> 47:59.500
this line, this line, and so on. And in particular, the set of lines that go through that point

48:00.140 --> 48:06.220
is the set of values m and b such that the yi's equal m xi's plus b.

48:09.340 --> 48:11.340
Which basically means that

48:14.380 --> 48:15.180
every point

48:18.700 --> 48:28.380
xy induces a set of possible values for the line that goes through that

48:30.300 --> 48:33.740
point. Specifically,

48:37.580 --> 48:43.740
the parameters of the line that go through that point are related through a linear equation.

48:43.740 --> 48:55.500
And in particular, the linear equation is b equals minus m xi plus y, which will be this.

48:55.500 --> 49:05.900
So a point has an associated line of parameters that could go through that

49:07.100 --> 49:10.540
point as a line. Does it make sense?

49:15.020 --> 49:18.540
So each one of these lines corresponds to a separate value of m and b.

49:19.420 --> 49:25.340
All those m, b values can be represented as a line in the m, b space.

49:27.180 --> 49:34.540
OK, so why is this a useful insight? Well, let's assume that now we have two points.

49:35.820 --> 49:40.220
So here we have xj, yj. So same idea here.

49:43.420 --> 49:46.300
There is an infinite amount of lines that can go through that point.

49:46.860 --> 49:54.540
But let's assume, but of course, there is only one line that goes through these two points.

49:56.460 --> 50:03.580
How will we detect that line in the m, b space? Well, let's assume that this is the

50:03.580 --> 50:12.700
m, b line associated with point i. And then we have another line that is associated with point

50:13.340 --> 50:18.860
j. Since there is only one line that goes through two points,

50:20.860 --> 50:26.780
the values of m, b of the line that goes through those two points is the intersection

50:27.900 --> 50:35.820
of the two lines in the m, b space. OK, so that's also an interesting insight.

50:35.900 --> 50:37.980
So any questions about this?

50:40.940 --> 50:47.260
So let's call m star, b star, the m, b parameters for this line.

50:47.820 --> 50:54.620
That will correspond to the coordinate of this point. So this will be m star. This will be b star.

50:57.100 --> 51:04.620
So the way that you use this intuition for line segmentation is as follows. That for each point,

51:06.620 --> 51:19.980
you have an induced set of m, b values. In particular, what you do is to grid the m, b space.

51:22.540 --> 51:32.220
And we say that each point votes for those m, b values that are possible for that point. So

51:32.220 --> 51:40.140
for example, for point i in the m, b grid, we will put a 1 here, a 1 here, a 1 here, a 1 here,

51:40.140 --> 51:45.340
1 here, 1 here, and so on. Because these are plausible parameters explaining the line going

51:45.340 --> 51:50.540
through that point. For this line here, it's too bad it's almost orthogonal. But actually,

51:50.540 --> 51:55.900
you will have one point here, one point here. Now, two points here, 1 plus 1, because both

51:56.700 --> 52:01.420
explain that line, 1, 1, 1, and so on. And of course, in general, you have many.

52:02.380 --> 52:09.500
And so you have accumulators. So each cell corresponds to an m, b value that is plausible

52:10.060 --> 52:16.140
for a given point. Here, we had the six points that had that value as plausible.

52:17.100 --> 52:23.260
So a typical result will be something like this. Now, you might say, well, but if you have

52:23.420 --> 52:32.300
multiple points, and all those points lie on the line, then you should have, for one cell,

52:32.300 --> 52:37.020
a very large number, and then ones, or very small numbers, for all the other ones.

52:37.020 --> 52:42.060
So why do we have this kind of fuzziness? Well, this is due, again, to measurement error,

52:42.620 --> 52:49.740
which means that the points, even if they are on the line in the real world, they may not be

52:49.740 --> 52:55.900
on the line in your image. So that's basically how, then, the hue transform approach for

52:55.900 --> 53:04.860
line segmentation works. Pick a point, you compute the m, b plausible values, and then you add the

53:04.860 --> 53:11.660
one for each cell in a discretized fashion that are intersected by that m, b line. And then you

53:11.660 --> 53:16.060
keep doing that for each point. Yeah? Can you decide the size of the

53:16.300 --> 53:25.020
error rate? That's a great question. The m is a tricky one, because it's a slope,

53:25.740 --> 53:30.300
so it's a tangent. So it goes back to, I think, the earlier question. This can go to infinity.

53:30.300 --> 53:36.060
So where do you stop? Tricky. And we see in a second how to address that.

53:36.060 --> 53:41.100
Because it's done the last 3D well, or? Oh, you mean for planes?

53:41.100 --> 53:42.700
Yeah. A little bit less.

53:43.660 --> 53:44.160
Yes.

53:46.940 --> 53:53.820
Going back to your question, let's go back to the polar equation of a line. Equivalently,

53:53.820 --> 54:04.380
a line can be written as x sinus of alpha plus y sinus of, sorry, x sinus of alpha plus

54:05.340 --> 54:16.700
x cosinus of alpha plus y sinus of alpha equal r. So given a point xi and yi,

54:18.540 --> 54:26.700
then the possible values alpha and r that define a line are related by a sinusoidal equation.

54:26.700 --> 54:35.740
So if I plot here, as depicted there, alpha and r

54:38.460 --> 54:51.020
as a body alpha, then I have a sinusoidal equation, and so on.

54:51.340 --> 54:55.740
So it's the same concept as before, but now instead of having a,

54:56.940 --> 55:04.460
so on each of these curves, we have a pair of alpha and r that represent a line that will go

55:04.460 --> 55:11.980
through the point xi and y. So same intuition, but while in nb space that the relation is a line,

55:11.980 --> 55:15.420
in the alpha and r space, this relation is a sinusoid.

55:15.420 --> 55:23.420
So wait, this is much better. Are you assuming that there's like an equal probability of lines

55:23.420 --> 55:29.660
having an alpha between 0 and 2 pi, whereas the probability of a line having an m decreases

55:29.660 --> 55:35.820
as m increases? Well, the thing is that with the m, you might have an m with very large values.

55:35.820 --> 55:39.340
Well, this one doesn't really make a difference. Alpha can be

55:40.300 --> 55:47.820
discretized with a constant resolution, and r is, if you remember, the distance of that line

55:47.820 --> 55:53.980
from the origin, and this is something that you can upper bound because you have an upper bound

55:53.980 --> 55:59.180
of the size of your environment. So you don't have any more this issue that one of the parameters

55:59.180 --> 56:07.180
will go to infinity. So you do the same thing, but now you have a domain for your grid that is much

56:08.140 --> 56:12.060
better well-defined. This goes back to your question earlier why I was considering

56:12.060 --> 56:16.300
polar coordinates. The third reason was exactly that specifically for Q-transform,

56:16.300 --> 56:19.980
that's the way to go in order to implement it. With xy, it wouldn't really work well.

56:21.260 --> 56:29.740
So then the algorithm is simple. So for each point, we have an induced sinusoidal line.

56:30.460 --> 56:36.860
So we take a discretization for the alpha angle, we compute the corresponding r value,

56:37.740 --> 56:44.780
as induced by the point xy and yi, and then we increase the accumulator, meaning the cell

56:44.780 --> 56:52.460
of the accumulator matrix H at coordinates alpha and r plus 1. And we do that for every

56:52.460 --> 56:57.340
discretization of alpha and, of course, for every point xy and yi that we have found. And then we

56:57.340 --> 57:05.500
choose alpha and r with the highest count. Let's look at an example. So here we have two lines,

57:06.380 --> 57:13.500
no clue where, so we don't know which point belongs to which line and even if we have a line.

57:13.500 --> 57:22.220
So for each point, we build a sinusoid in alpha r space. And as you can see here, we find the two

57:22.220 --> 57:29.180
intersections. These two intersections correspond to the alpha and r of the two lines, which is

57:29.180 --> 57:37.980
exactly what we have here reconstructed. Now in reality, the situation usually is a bit less

57:37.980 --> 57:44.300
clean because, of course, we have noise. So that would be the situation in alpha and r space. So

57:44.300 --> 57:48.620
we don't really have a clean intersection, but usually it's rather easy to find a cluster that

57:48.620 --> 57:53.740
really corresponds to an intersection. So this algorithm is quite neat because, as you see,

57:53.740 --> 58:02.620
we didn't have to make any complicated indexing policy or threshold choices and so on. So that's

58:02.620 --> 58:08.700
why this algorithm is quite widely used in practice. Robust, easy to implement and so on.

58:10.700 --> 58:15.020
You had two peaks and you could see that probably it's two lines,

58:15.020 --> 58:23.100
just one side or how many peaks you're expecting. No, in principle, you don't know how many

58:23.100 --> 58:27.740
peaks to expect. In general, yes, you have an upper bound. I mean, in a reasonable

58:27.740 --> 58:32.220
structural environment, it will be difficult to have one trillion lines. But

58:32.780 --> 58:37.340
no, in principle, this algorithm finds all the lines that are out there.

58:37.980 --> 58:42.220
So you would set a threshold for the peak and then choose a peak based on that?

