01:06:05.260 --> 01:06:06.380
Is that clear from last time?

01:06:08.380 --> 01:06:10.460
So you can rewrite,

01:06:10.460 --> 01:06:12.380
that's what also you will do in your pset,

01:06:13.020 --> 01:06:16.540
the correlation as the dot product between two vectors.

01:06:16.540 --> 01:06:18.860
And the idea is relatively simple.

01:06:18.860 --> 01:06:19.980
So what you do is...

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:30.380
So say that you have a matrix f

01:06:32.860 --> 01:06:38.700
with rows f1, f2, fn.

01:06:40.060 --> 01:06:45.660
Then you can form a vector f equal to f1, f2,

01:06:46.380 --> 01:06:49.500
whereby essentially you concatenate the rows.

01:06:54.460 --> 01:06:56.540
For the pixel neighborhood,

01:06:56.540 --> 01:07:02.460
so the patch of the image i centered at the pixel x, y,

01:07:02.460 --> 01:07:04.220
whereas for the neighborhoods i, j,

01:07:04.220 --> 01:07:08.460
where the i, j is the neighborhood of the image,

01:07:08.460 --> 01:07:09.340
you can do the same thing.

01:07:09.340 --> 01:07:12.220
From the matrix you can put it in a vector form.

01:07:12.780 --> 01:07:15.420
Now the reason why here we have a subscript

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:25.180
is that this vector depends on the index of the pixel clash notation.

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:27.660
I should have put a subscript here, x and y.

01:07:28.540 --> 01:07:30.620
While in your pset you have i, j.

01:07:30.620 --> 01:07:31.420
Sorry for that.

01:07:31.420 --> 01:07:35.980
So to be consistent with this notation, t subscript x and y,

01:07:35.980 --> 01:07:38.460
because this vector depends on the pixel

01:07:39.260 --> 01:07:41.100
at which currently your mask is centered.

01:07:42.940 --> 01:07:45.340
And this vector obviously depends on that pixel.

01:07:45.820 --> 01:07:51.980
So then you can just rewrite this linear combination as a dot product.

01:07:51.980 --> 01:07:55.660
Remember that the dot product is the sum of the products

01:07:55.660 --> 01:07:59.180
of the components of these vectors.

01:08:02.140 --> 01:08:03.180
So this is a dot product.

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.660
So the intuition of a dot product, if you will,

01:08:05.660 --> 01:08:08.060
is to see alignments between two vectors.

01:08:09.340 --> 01:08:12.460
So in particular, if the two vectors are normalized,

01:08:12.540 --> 01:08:16.460
the dot product is maximum when these two vectors are equal.

01:08:17.900 --> 01:08:21.980
Which basically induces us to consider the problem of object detection

01:08:21.980 --> 01:08:24.540
as a problem of normalized correlation.

01:08:24.540 --> 01:08:29.180
Whereby we do the correlation for simplicity just expressed as a dot product,

01:08:29.180 --> 01:08:36.940
but where we normalize it in order that if the neighborhood around x and y,

01:08:36.940 --> 01:08:42.380
so this y, j here, looks exactly like the mask f,

01:08:43.020 --> 01:08:44.620
then this dot product is equal to 1.

01:08:46.380 --> 01:08:50.940
Which has the intuition that the feature that has the notion of a template

01:08:50.940 --> 01:08:53.500
exactly looks like the neighborhood around x, y,

01:08:53.500 --> 01:08:55.260
which means that we have found Waldo.

01:08:55.260 --> 01:08:55.980
Does it make sense?

01:09:00.140 --> 01:09:01.100
Any questions about it?

01:09:01.100 --> 01:09:04.460
Can we compose filters of different scale and location?

01:09:05.100 --> 01:09:06.140
Yep, in a second.

01:09:06.940 --> 01:09:10.380
So that will be the result.

01:09:10.380 --> 01:09:13.420
So you do this sliding.

01:09:14.380 --> 01:09:17.340
So you slide this template across the full image,

01:09:18.620 --> 01:09:21.660
and then, ta-da, you have a peak.

01:09:22.540 --> 01:09:23.980
And that's where you put your bounding box.

01:09:24.620 --> 01:09:26.940
In fact, I mean, this is not PPT.

01:09:26.940 --> 01:09:28.620
This is done for real.

01:09:29.820 --> 01:09:30.620
This was Waldo.

01:09:32.380 --> 01:09:34.380
And that's exactly where we found the peak.

01:09:35.740 --> 01:09:36.380
Isn't that neat?

01:09:37.500 --> 01:09:40.300
Also, it's a nice application of linear filtering.

01:09:40.300 --> 01:09:44.380
So it allows you to appreciate what the linear filters are

01:09:44.380 --> 01:09:47.900
and also very clever use of linear filtering

01:09:47.900 --> 01:09:49.180
in order to do object detection.

01:09:49.820 --> 01:09:55.020
Now, the problem here is that we have basically assumed

01:09:56.780 --> 01:10:01.260
that we more or less know the scale of the object that we're looking for.

01:10:03.100 --> 01:10:06.460
But what if I get all of a sudden much closer to the image,

01:10:06.460 --> 01:10:08.780
meaning that the Waldo becomes much larger?

01:10:08.780 --> 01:10:13.500
Now I have a template that is much smaller

01:10:13.500 --> 01:10:15.340
than the object I'm trying to detect.

01:10:15.980 --> 01:10:18.140
In that case, I would not have detection.

01:10:20.460 --> 01:10:21.900
How do we address that?

01:10:21.900 --> 01:10:23.500
Well, the idea is fairly easy.

01:10:24.380 --> 01:10:30.220
The idea is, in order to address this problem of sizing,

01:10:30.220 --> 01:10:33.500
we just rescale an image up and down

01:10:34.380 --> 01:10:36.060
according to many different scales.

01:10:36.540 --> 01:10:39.580
And we do template matching with respect to each scale.

01:10:40.380 --> 01:10:42.620
And if we have a match at a given scale,

01:10:42.620 --> 01:10:46.220
then we say that we have found an object.

01:10:46.780 --> 01:10:49.740
This idea gives rise to the idea of image pyramids

01:10:49.740 --> 01:10:53.580
because we have a pyramidal structure of images,

01:10:53.580 --> 01:10:55.900
one per scale, like here.

01:10:55.900 --> 01:10:56.940
View this as a pyramid.

01:10:59.340 --> 01:11:01.020
So this means that we want to both,

01:11:01.020 --> 01:11:04.060
so we have an image, we want to downscale it

01:11:04.060 --> 01:11:05.580
and we want to upscale it.

01:11:05.580 --> 01:11:09.340
In the hope that at some scale we have the...

01:11:09.980 --> 01:11:10.540
Is it cute?

01:11:10.540 --> 01:11:11.900
Now you can see in a second why it is cute.

01:11:13.020 --> 01:11:16.220
It means that at some scale, if there is an object,

01:11:16.220 --> 01:11:17.500
you will find it with a template.

01:11:18.940 --> 01:11:21.020
How do we subsample an image?

01:11:21.020 --> 01:11:25.660
So possible way of doing it will be just to keep

01:11:25.660 --> 01:11:27.820
only some rows and some columns.

01:11:27.820 --> 01:11:29.420
Like for example, if I give you this image

01:11:30.140 --> 01:11:33.340
where there is a small chicken and this is a mouse.

01:11:33.340 --> 01:11:33.980
How do we call it?

01:11:34.860 --> 01:11:37.100
Mouse, yeah, cute mouse.

01:11:39.260 --> 01:11:41.660
Say that you want to subsample in such a way

01:11:41.660 --> 01:11:43.580
that we reduce the width by half.

01:11:43.580 --> 01:11:46.140
You might say, okay, we remove every other column.

01:11:47.420 --> 01:11:50.300
Well, if you do that, the chicken is gone.

01:11:52.940 --> 01:11:54.620
Because basically the way that it worked

01:11:55.180 --> 01:11:56.940
is that every other line,

01:11:56.940 --> 01:11:58.940
there was the overlaid image of the chicken

01:11:58.940 --> 01:12:01.740
and every other line, the image of the mouse.

01:12:02.620 --> 01:12:04.780
If I just remove every other line,

01:12:04.780 --> 01:12:07.500
the ones that were giving you the chicken,

01:12:07.500 --> 01:12:08.300
then that is gone.

01:12:08.940 --> 01:12:10.060
So we miss information.

01:12:11.660 --> 01:12:12.620
So what's happening here?

01:12:13.820 --> 01:12:18.540
That in this picture, I had very high frequency components.

01:12:18.540 --> 01:12:20.540
These are the factors that was constantly changing,

01:12:21.420 --> 01:12:23.340
interlacing the image of the chicken

01:12:23.340 --> 01:12:24.940
with the image of the mouse.

01:12:24.940 --> 01:12:27.180
So if you're familiar with signal processing,

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:28.860
then you are familiar with the notion

01:12:28.860 --> 01:12:30.860
of a Nyquist frequency.

01:12:30.860 --> 01:12:35.900
Basically you don't want to sample at a frequency

01:12:35.900 --> 01:12:37.820
that creates the aliasing.

01:12:37.820 --> 01:12:39.420
And this is exactly what is happening here.

01:12:40.540 --> 01:12:42.300
So if you're familiar with signal processing,

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:44.780
in order to avoid this problem, what will you do?

01:12:45.740 --> 01:12:47.980
So here the problem is that we have frequencies

01:12:47.980 --> 01:12:50.300
that were too high for this subsampling procedure.

01:12:52.140 --> 01:12:57.420
So what you do is basically to remove those high frequencies

01:12:58.140 --> 01:13:00.380
in such a way that the subsampling is more robust.

01:13:01.260 --> 01:13:04.780
How do you basically remove high frequencies?

01:13:06.700 --> 01:13:09.420
So for example, we already looked at this when we removed noise.

01:13:09.420 --> 01:13:12.220
Noise can be seen as basically high frequency content in the image.

01:13:12.220 --> 01:13:14.140
To remove it, we blurred it.

01:13:14.140 --> 01:13:14.780
Yeah?

01:13:14.780 --> 01:13:18.540
Did you put a filter that caps off at a certain frequency or something?

01:13:18.540 --> 01:13:20.940
Yeah, which is basically what Gaussian Blur does.

01:13:22.140 --> 01:13:27.260
That basically smoothing out so that the frequency content is...

01:13:27.260 --> 01:13:28.940
Again, if you are familiar with signal processing,

01:13:28.940 --> 01:13:29.740
this is fairly obvious.

01:13:29.740 --> 01:13:31.340
If you are not familiar with signal processing,

01:13:31.340 --> 01:13:32.460
this is not so obvious.

01:13:33.100 --> 01:13:36.060
But at least visually, this is what happens.

01:13:36.060 --> 01:13:37.980
We are given again the picture as before.

01:13:38.860 --> 01:13:41.820
But now we do Gaussian Blurring first,

01:13:42.460 --> 01:13:46.620
which means that it smears out the high frequency content.

01:13:46.620 --> 01:13:49.420
That's how it will look like with Gaussian Blurring.

01:13:49.420 --> 01:13:52.300
Now with subsample, and that's what we'll get.

01:13:52.300 --> 01:13:52.940
Much better.

01:13:54.940 --> 01:13:58.140
So this means that in order to subsample,

01:13:58.140 --> 01:13:59.420
what you have to do is fairly simple.

01:13:59.980 --> 01:14:02.860
You first blur your image with a Gaussian filter,

01:14:02.860 --> 01:14:05.980
and then you keep every other column or every other row.

01:14:06.780 --> 01:14:09.100
And that's something that you will do in the pset as well.

01:14:09.740 --> 01:14:11.260
So subsampling is easy.

01:14:12.620 --> 01:14:14.620
Upsampling is also relatively easy,

01:14:14.620 --> 01:14:17.340
but now what you have to do is to do interpolation.

01:14:17.980 --> 01:14:20.540
And there are a number of ways to do interpolation,

01:14:20.540 --> 01:14:23.740
like a cubic interpolation, nearest neighbor, bilinear.

01:14:23.740 --> 01:14:25.180
Bilinear is a particularly good one.

01:14:26.540 --> 01:14:29.420
We're not going to go into the details of interpolation.

01:14:29.820 --> 01:14:32.620
But the key point is that interpolation can be seen

01:14:32.620 --> 01:14:34.460
as a linear filtering procedure,

01:14:34.460 --> 01:14:37.260
and that's exactly what you will do in the pset.

01:14:37.260 --> 01:14:40.460
So in the pset, basically, you would use subsampling, upsampling.

01:14:42.540 --> 01:14:44.700
Actually, not all of you, just the graduate students.

01:14:44.700 --> 01:14:45.820
This is the extra problem.

01:14:46.780 --> 01:14:49.820
So sorry, you and I, it's not for you.

01:14:49.820 --> 01:14:51.100
But you can always do it.

01:14:51.100 --> 01:14:51.820
It's kind of fun.

01:14:54.220 --> 01:14:55.420
And now that you have pyramids,

01:14:55.420 --> 01:14:57.980
then you will do template matching with respect to each scale.

01:15:00.540 --> 01:15:04.860
Okay, this is one way of doing object detection.

01:15:05.820 --> 01:15:08.300
But this basically assumes that you know what you're looking for,

01:15:08.300 --> 01:15:10.140
and you have a template for what you're looking for.

01:15:10.940 --> 01:15:15.740
A much more powerful approach is the so-called bags of visual words.

01:15:15.740 --> 01:15:18.700
And it can be quick, quick in terms of two-minute quick.

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:28.060
So the idea of the bag of visual words is that we interpret images

01:15:28.060 --> 01:15:34.460
as a distribution of features, also referred to as visual words.

01:15:34.460 --> 01:15:36.700
The reason why we call them visual words

01:15:36.700 --> 01:15:39.580
is because this technique is very much related to document retrieval.

01:15:40.140 --> 01:15:45.980
So in particular, the idea is that we have a dictionary of basic features

01:15:45.980 --> 01:15:47.740
that you might find in a picture.

01:15:48.860 --> 01:15:52.620
Then what we do is that once we have a picture at query time,

01:15:53.500 --> 01:16:00.220
we look at the frequency whereby each one of these possible visual words appear.

01:16:00.220 --> 01:16:02.540
So for example, this one appears a lot,

01:16:02.540 --> 01:16:04.860
this doesn't appear much, and so on.

01:16:04.860 --> 01:16:07.420
So then what we do is we take this histogram,

01:16:07.420 --> 01:16:10.940
we compare it with the histograms of images

01:16:10.940 --> 01:16:14.140
that we have in a database that we have used for training,

01:16:14.140 --> 01:16:15.500
and we find the closest match.

01:16:18.860 --> 01:16:20.620
There are a lot of things, of course,

01:16:20.620 --> 01:16:23.820
how do I come up with this dictionary,

01:16:23.820 --> 01:16:27.740
how do I do the comparison with the images in the database

01:16:29.180 --> 01:16:31.660
in a feature way, and so on and so forth.

01:16:31.660 --> 01:16:33.580
We're not going to go into the details of that.

01:16:33.580 --> 01:16:34.940
But this is just to say that,

01:16:34.940 --> 01:16:39.180
and this was one of the most popular algorithms for object recognition.

01:16:39.180 --> 01:16:41.740
Again, the idea, just a high-level idea,

01:16:41.740 --> 01:16:46.220
is to view an image as a bunch of features

01:16:46.220 --> 01:16:48.060
with both a detector and a descriptor,

01:16:48.700 --> 01:16:57.420
and then form an histogram that gives the frequencies of each feature

01:16:57.420 --> 01:17:00.380
as a store in a given dictionary.

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:02.860
Of course, this dictionary should be large enough

01:17:02.860 --> 01:17:05.500
in order to account for all the possible things

01:17:05.500 --> 01:17:07.420
that you could see in the world, but not too large.

01:17:07.420 --> 01:17:10.780
Otherwise, it becomes very difficult to do a match.

01:17:11.420 --> 01:17:14.540
And then you do matching with a set of images

01:17:14.540 --> 01:17:17.580
that you have in a database that become your reference images

01:17:17.580 --> 01:17:18.940
in order to do classification.

01:17:19.660 --> 01:17:20.860
Does it make sense, more or less?

01:17:23.420 --> 01:17:25.500
So this was basically the de facto standard

01:17:25.500 --> 01:17:29.660
for object classification until about seven, eight years ago,

01:17:29.660 --> 01:17:30.540
which is kind of amazing.

01:17:30.540 --> 01:17:33.420
When I was doing my PhD, that was like this thing,

01:17:33.980 --> 01:17:37.900
but now everything has been overtaken by neural nets.

01:17:37.900 --> 01:17:39.980
So not just for future description,

01:17:40.620 --> 01:17:44.700
but also, and most importantly, for recognition.

01:17:44.700 --> 01:17:46.780
The interesting thing of neural nets

01:17:46.780 --> 01:17:49.900
is that many of the concepts that we talked about,

01:17:49.900 --> 01:17:53.100
like template matching, representing figures

01:17:53.100 --> 01:17:59.580
as frequency histograms of basis visual words and so on,

01:17:59.580 --> 01:18:02.700
are implicitly learned by a neural network.

01:18:02.700 --> 01:18:05.900
So it's not that a neural network does something completely different

01:18:05.900 --> 01:18:07.820
from what it was done before.

01:18:07.820 --> 01:18:10.860
It's just that what people were painfully doing

01:18:10.860 --> 01:18:14.860
in terms of coming up with all the right templates and so on

01:18:14.940 --> 01:18:19.660
is now basically implicitly encoded through learning

01:18:19.660 --> 01:18:20.700
into a neural network.

01:18:22.060 --> 01:18:25.100
To the point that now this is the most reliable thing

01:18:25.100 --> 01:18:27.180
that you can use in order for object recognition,

01:18:27.180 --> 01:18:29.580
and indeed what you will be using for your final project.

01:18:29.580 --> 01:18:31.020
As I said, as a black box.

01:18:31.020 --> 01:18:33.740
So for you, it will be just an input-output box.

01:18:33.740 --> 01:18:36.300
You put an image in, you get a category out.

01:18:37.020 --> 01:18:38.620
The details will be into 74p.

01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:42.860
All right, so this was the last lecture for robot perception.

01:18:42.860 --> 01:18:45.420
I hope that I give you a good overview

01:18:45.420 --> 01:18:47.500
of what robot perception entails

01:18:47.500 --> 01:18:50.380
in terms of 3D world reconstruction,

01:18:50.380 --> 01:18:51.500
how a camera works,

01:18:51.500 --> 01:18:53.660
how you extract information about basic ones,

01:18:53.660 --> 01:18:55.580
axiomatic primitives, and more advanced ones.

01:18:56.220 --> 01:19:03.820
On Tuesday next week, we'll talk about localization.

01:19:03.820 --> 01:19:08.220
So the first step to do so will be to give an introduction

01:19:08.220 --> 01:19:10.620
to filtering theory, and then we will use that

01:19:10.620 --> 01:19:13.340
in order to solve the problem localization and mapping.

01:19:13.340 --> 01:19:14.780
Tomorrow, for the graduate students,

01:19:14.780 --> 01:19:19.020
will be a more advanced lecture

01:19:19.020 --> 01:19:21.260
on modern computer vision techniques.

01:19:22.540 --> 01:19:25.500
All right, see you tomorrow.

